Test libpcre2-8-0 @ 10.46 for compatibility with a regular expression to match "an IPv4 address".

Be sure to read the LICENCE. (eg. 'cat LICENCE', 'nano LICENCE', 'grep LICENCE'...);

Be sure to read the CONTRIB(utors credits). (eg. 'cat CONTRIB', 'nano CONTRIB', 'grep CONTRIB'...);

run ./configure
run ./make [install]
run ./build/pcre_test OR if ./make install; run ./usr/bin/pcre_test

I made this project because grep has not worked (for me) for quite a while now,
as reported in Debian Bug #1106335. The failures occur with various regex
patterns: this small test proves that the problem is not libpcre2, but
something else.

The following shell script best illustrates my problem:

```sh
    PATH_TEST="../dir/dir/dir/dir/@#$%^&*()-_=+\\|;:'\",<.>/?/";
    echo "$PATH_TEST" | \
    grep -o --color=always "^\.\?\.\?\(/[a-zA-Z0-9@#$%^&*()_=+\|;:'\",<>/?-]\+\)\+/\?$";
```

The output should be equal to PATH_TEST. (at least, on Debian 13) It is not.
